# Governed Delivery (.protocol)

¶ - Full lifecycle policy for the TDSL spec-driven workflow. Routed from the global policy ¶11; read only when entering or auditing the flow.

document:
  id: task.sdd-policy@1
  version: 1
  status: proposed
  authority_basis: [@task/global-policy @arch/tdsl-sdd @arch/tdsl-language]
  scope: lifecycle, layout, authority, dag, freshness, controlled_return, commands
  excludes: dialect_field_types, phase_authoring_procedure, assurance_selection, acceptance_records # skills and schemas own these; assurance and acceptance are deliberate omissions
  entry skill(protocol)

¶ 1 — Backbone

```text
initialize
-> constitution
-> feature
-> specify
-> plan
-> tasks
-> analyze
-> execute
-> close   # ends the cycle; see ¶10
-> idle    # nothing governed is open; the next step is the operator's
```

Each phase writes one record. No phase asks permission to begin: it needs its
predecessors to exist because it references their parts, which is referential
integrity, not authorization.

¶ 2 — Layout

```text
.protocol/project.tdsl                              # anchor: cursor + lifecycle register, ¶10
.protocol/.scaffold/<artifact>.tdsl                 # authoring skeletons, ¶11; never a record
.protocol/constitution.tdsl                         # constitution@1
.protocol/features/<NNN-name>/spec.tdsl             # spec@1
.protocol/features/<NNN-name>/plan.tdsl             # plan@1
.protocol/features/<NNN-name>/tasks.tdsl            # tasks@1
.protocol/features/<NNN-name>/analysis.tdsl         # analysis@1
.protocol/features/<NNN-name>/decomposition.tdsl    # optional, ¶12; absence is not a gap
.protocol/features/<NNN-name>/execution.journal.tdsl # runtime, never accepted

dialects [constitution@1 spec@1 plan@1 tasks@1 analysis@1]
schema_lock {{KERNEL_HOME}}/skills/_shared/schema-lock.tdsl
field_contracts {{KERNEL_HOME}}/skills/_shared/field-contracts.tdsl
```

¶ 3 — Records, not authorities

```text
artifact a record of a decision already taken in conversation
  has no acceptance status
  has no pass or fail
  passes no judgment on itself
  ban asking an artifact whether it is good; it has no opinion

operator present in the conversation; reviews as the work is produced
  silence is agreement; a change is stated, not recorded as a rejection
  ban a mechanism that reconstructs, from files, a fact the transcript already holds

analyze the only tool that emits a verdict, and the verdict is advice
  it reports contradiction, ambiguity, gap and drift
  it never withholds a transition; the operator decides what matters

gate identity, sequence, conformance, coherence, refusal on a broken reference
  ban the gate granting or withholding permission
```

Acceptance and lifecycle are different things, and only one of them is banned
from the records:

```text
acceptance a judgement of quality
  belongs to the operator, who is in the conversation
  the transcript already holds it; a file would attest nothing
  ban an artifact carrying accepted, approved, pass or fail

lifecycle position in the cycle
  is this feature still the one that routes work
  is it finished, and if so with what standing
  the conversation ended and the next agent enters cold; the repository is the
    only thing still speaking to it, so position must be written down
  ban a lifecycle value that expresses merit rather than position
```

The distinction is what makes `records_have_no_status` survive: a lifecycle
field describes where the work sits, never how good it is.

Why there is no acceptance layer: LIMA needs one because a kernel governs
execution where the operator is genuinely absent at decision time, so a record
must speak for them. Here the operator is in the room. An acceptance record
would attest what the conversation already contains, and the agent runs the
command that writes it — so it attests nothing and costs friction.

This package deliberately omits two upstream concepts: independent assurance
selection (tdsl-sdd ¶14) and acceptance record contracts (tdsl-sdd ¶13). Assurance
as an independent policy gate does not fit an operator-in-room model where
coherence findings are presented and the operator decides. Acceptance records are
unnecessary because silence in transcript is agreement; a file-based record would
duplicate what the conversation already holds.

¶ 4 — Invariants

```text
sequence_before_author a phase needs its predecessors to exist; the gate refuses a broken reference
one_phase_one_skill a phase skeleton is loaded only by its own skill
records_have_no_status an artifact never carries acceptance, pass, or fail
verdict_is_advisory analyze reports; the operator decides; nothing is withheld
unknown_is_not_assumption material uncertainty -> explicit_unknowns
one_proposition_one_location downstream artifacts reference upstream ids, never restate them
runtime_is_not_planning progress lives in the execution journal, never in a planning record
optional_only_when_material an absent optional section beats an empty ceremonial one
lifecycle_is_position_not_merit a feature records where it sits, never how good it is
one_cursor_many_lives active_feature points at one feature; the register holds every feature's position
closing_is_an_operator_act the agent proposes a closing state; the operator directs it; close records it
completing_one_cycle_opens_none delivery is not a reason to start the next feature
```

¶ 5 — Derivation and drift

```text
constitution -> [spec plan tasks analysis]
spec -> [plan tasks analysis]
plan -> [tasks analysis]
tasks -> analysis
```

Drift is detected by content, not by byte digest. Editing a spec does not stale
anything; it may make a downstream record incoherent, and that is what analyze
reports:

```text
add a requirement -> `requirement_unproved` until some validation proves it
rename a part -> `unresolved_reference` wherever the old id is still cited
remove a component -> `unresolved_reference` in every task that referenced it
reword without changing meaning -> nothing, correctly
```

This replaces the digest-based freshness of `tdsl-sdd ¶5`. The upstream can
compare semantic `ir_digest`; this package has no IR, and a byte digest would
have flagged a comma as a semantic change. Content coherence is both cheaper and
closer to what the rule meant. Declared divergence, not an omission.

¶ 6 — Controlled Return

```text
defect_found -> return to the artifact owning the semantic defect
ban mutating an unrelated predecessor to make a downstream phase pass
ban inferring an operator answer to close an explicit_unknown
during_delivery a defect with a known answer is fixed through problem_triage; only a real unknown returns to the operator
semantic_change -> new version, renewed acceptance, recalculated staleness
preserve current, rejected and replacement versions
attempt_count telemetry, never authority
```

¶ 7 — Commands

```text
state   python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/sdd_gate.py state
init    python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/sdd_gate.py init --name <project>
feature python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/sdd_gate.py feature <NNN-kebab-name> [--reopen]
close   python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/sdd_gate.py close <id> --as <state> [--successor <id>] [--reason <text>]
analyze python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/sdd_gate.py analyze # coerência entre registros
gate    python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/sdd_gate.py gate <phase>
check   python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/sdd_gate.py check <path.tdsl>
trail   python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/sdd_gate.py trail # o que existe, o que analyze achou, o que falta
repin   python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/tdsl_lock.py # after any grammar schema change
```

¶ 8 — Skills

```text
protocol     router and state reader, lifecycle entry point; owns feature, close and idle
constitution phase(constitution) -> constitution@1
specify      phase(specify) -> spec@1
clarify      phase(clarify) -> spec@1 next version, governed replacement
plan         phase(plan) -> plan@1
tasks        phase(tasks) -> tasks@1
analyze      phase(analyze) -> analysis@1, read_only
execute      phase(execute) -> implementation files + execution journal
```

Analysis runs deterministically before it judges:

```text
python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/sdd_gate.py analyze

settles_mechanically:
  requirement_unproved, unresolved_reference, malformed_reference
  component_unexercised, criterion_unmeasured, explicit_unknown, not_accepted_fresh

remains_judgment:
  policy_alignment, duplication, drift, silent_scope
  atomicity, verifiability, authority, runtime_leak
```

¶ 9 — Legacy Coexistence

Here `legacy` means the regime that came before governance — markdown specs under
docs/specs/ — never a component this package retired. The retired script is named
`legacy_archive_spec.py` in that same sense: it belongs to that regime.

```text
project_has(.protocol) -> this policy owns spec, plan and tasks; skill(spec) is not used
project_lacks(.protocol) -> markdown specs under docs/specs/active/ via skill(spec)
adoption operator decides; discovery is read-only and mutates nothing
ban rewriting repository history to adopt governance
```

¶ 10 — Lifecycle

A feature has two independent facts about it, and one slot could never hold
both. `active_feature` is a cursor: which feature this session is pointed at,
one at most, moved freely. The register says where each feature sits.

```text
.protocol/project.tdsl
  active_feature: <id> | unknown        # cursor, zero or one
  features:                             # register, every feature the project has
    feature <id>:
      lifecycle: <state>
      successor: <id>                   # required when superseded
      closed_at: <YYYY-MM-DD>
      reason: <text>                    # required when abandoned
```

```text
active     in work; the only state a feature routes work from
delivered  finished; its invariants still hold; consult it, do not route from it
heritage   finished and explicitly material to future work; reuse it actively
historical finished; kept as evidence and rationale; consult, do not reuse
superseded replaced by another feature, which is named; a claim without a named
             successor is a classification with no evidence
  ban naming itself as successor; that records no substitution
  ban an abandoned successor; work is not replaced by work that never shipped
  ban a succession chain that closes on itself
abandoned  ended without delivery, with the reason recorded
```

`heritage` and `historical` both mean "does not route". They differ in the
licence they carry: heritage says reuse this, historical says this is why. Without
the distinction, "no normative authority" gets read as "no use", and reusable
work is rebuilt from nothing.

```text
open   `feature <id>` registers the feature as active and points the cursor at it
close  `close <id> --as <state>` records where it ended and clears the cursor if it pointed there
reopen `feature <id> --reopen` puts a closed feature back in work, dropping the
         closing facts that no longer hold; it rewrites no history
idle   cursor empty or pointing at a closed feature -> next_phase is idle
```

```text
ban a lifecycle state that expresses quality
ban the agent closing a feature on its own initiative; it proposes, the operator directs
ban an argument outside the state that requires it: successor only with superseded,
  reason only with abandoned. A delivered feature carrying a closing reason implies
  a justification for ending that its own state says does not exist.
ban treating delivery as authorization to open the next feature
ban directories as classification: `.protocol/archive/<state>/` would make the path
  assert a fact nobody verified, and reclassifying would become a file move plus
  every cross reference that pointed at the old path. The state is a field that
  is read; the layout stays features/<id>/.
```

Compatibility: an anchor written before the register existed has no `features:`
block. Its active feature reads as `active` and nothing fails. A project does not
stop working for lacking a field that did not exist when it was written.

¶ 11 — Scaffold

The structure of a governed project is always the same; only the content differs.
`init` places every authoring skeleton in one pass, and a phase fills one in
rather than deriving the shape again.

```text
path .protocol/.scaffold/<artifact>.tdsl
source {{KERNEL_HOME}}/skills/<phase>/template.tdsl
refresh python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/<gate>.py scaffold # after a harness update

rule the skeleton is a shape, never a record
rule it lives outside every path the gate resolves an artifact from
  reason next_phase decides by existence; a skeleton sitting where an artifact
    belongs would make a freshly initialized project report itself ready to execute
rule it carries placeholders and directives, which `check` refuses by design
ban the hook gating a write to .scaffold/; there is no phase to gate
```

¶ 12 — Decomposition

```text
path .protocol/features/<NNN-name>/decomposition.tdsl
dialect local:decomposition@1 # local; the kernel has no subtask contract
declared_divergence its rules live in Python (check_decomposition), not in generated
  TDSL, because tdsl_lock.py emits contracts from the bundled grammar schemas and the kernel
  has no subtask dialect to emit. Deliberate, not an oversight; if the kernel ever
  grows one, this moves to field-contracts and the check becomes a dialect lookup.
skill decompose
optional absent by default; absence is never a finding and never a phase the router asks for

propose_when one task holds more than one independently validated result
operator decides; the agent names the task and says why
sibling_not_nested subtasks are their own container, with an explicit parent
  reason the shared block parser reads fields exactly one level below an item, so a
    subtask nested inside a task is dropped in silence
depends_on orders subtasks of one parent only; ordering between tasks is the task DAG's
  reason a second ordering model is how two contradictory orders come to exist
```

¶ 13 — Planning Modes

The operator chooses how far planning runs ahead of code. The gate never forces
either; it records the choice and routes accordingly.

```text
mode gradual # default: one feature runs specify -> plan -> tasks -> analyze -> execute, then the next opens
mode integral # every active feature is specified, planned, tasked and analysed before any code
record anchor field `planning_mode`, set by `init --mode` or `mode <value>`
route integral -> next_phase targets the first active feature still missing a planning record
view roadmap -> per-feature planning table; "all features planned" marks the start of execution
invariant sequence_before_author still holds per feature; integral changes order across features, never within one
choose integral when architecture must be settled whole before code; gradual when learning from each delivery matters
```

```text
integral_package: # how the planning session ends in integral mode
  1 author every feature: spec, plan, tasks, as many as the work needs
  2 spawn ONE analysis subagent over the whole package, every active feature at once
    it writes each feature's analysis.tdsl; cross-feature findings route to the owning record
  3 fix what the findings require, through problem_triage; the operator sees only real unknowns
  4 package ready -> the planning session stops; execution is a separate session the operator opens
ban analysing feature by feature in integral mode; contradictions between features are what it exists to catch
```

¶ 14 — Delivery Session

The operator opens an execution session only when the whole package is ready.
The session therefore takes the package as already inspected and executes in
order; re-inspecting it would repeat paid work and invite re-planning mid-build.

```text
precondition every active feature holds spec, plan, tasks and analysis
premise package already inspected
ban initial analysis, initial test run, or presenting coherence findings at session start
trail checks that records exist; nothing else is re-judged

roles:
  orchestrator the main session; holds project intent; reads state, records and reports only
    never edits source
    classifies every validation by test-triage (constitution, else skill test-protocol) before any test exists
  test_author one subagent per feature; writes tests from spec, plan and independent references,
    before implementation, never reading implementation code
  executor one subagent per feature; implements tasks until the tests pass; keeps the journal
    ban editing or deleting a test
    test believed wrong -> return it to the orchestrator with the reason
  rule one feature in flight; the next spawns only after the orchestrator closes the current one

loop:
  1 python {{KERNEL_HOME}}/scripts/sdd_gate.py state
  2 orchestrator states the test-triage class of every validation of the active plan
  3 spawn test_author with the validations that need tests; tests land, failing
  4 spawn executor with tasks.tdsl, the test paths and the ban on test edits
  5 orchestrator runs the build check and the whole suite once, judges the result against
    the scope of the whole feature through problem_triage, then returns precise failures to
    the same executor or proceeds
  6 every required validation passes -> close <id> --as delivered when the constitution
    authority pre-directs it; otherwise propose the state and wait
  7 feature <next-id> moves the cursor; back to 1
  8 register exhausted -> report to the operator and stop

stop only on a constitution stop_condition, an explicit_unknown that blocks the active task,
  or a production dependency awaiting operator confirmation
ban asking the operator between features
ban opening spikes, comparative tests or validation rounds the plan does not declare
```
